## Solution

We evaluate
$$I=\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)\,d\theta$$
by the residue theorem.

**Step 1: Encode the integrand as a real part.** Since $\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)=\Re\!\bigl[e^{i(\theta+\cos\theta)}\bigr]$,

$$e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)=\Re\!\Bigl[e^{\sin\theta+i\cos\theta}\,e^{i\theta}\Bigr].$$

The key observation is
$$\sin\theta+i\cos\theta=i(\cos\theta-i\sin\theta)=i\,e^{-i\theta},$$
so $e^{\sin\theta+i\cos\theta}=e^{i e^{-i\theta}}$. Hence
$$I=\Re\!\left(\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}e^{i e^{-i\theta}}\,e^{i\theta}\,d\theta\right)=\Re\,J.$$

**Step 2: Convert to a contour integral.** Set $z=e^{i\theta}$, so $d\theta=\dfrac{dz}{iz}$, $e^{i\theta}=z$, and $e^{-i\theta}=1/z$. As $\theta$ runs from $-\pi$ to $\pi$, $z$ traverses the unit circle $|z|=1$ once counterclockwise. Thus
$$J=\oint_{|z|=1} e^{i/z}\,z\,\frac{dz}{iz}=\frac{1}{i}\oint_{|z|=1}e^{i/z}\,dz.$$

**Step 3: Compute the residue.** Expand $e^{i/z}$ in a Laurent series about $z=0$:
$$e^{i/z}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(i/z)^{n}}{n!}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{i^{n}}{n!}\,z^{-n}.$$
The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ (the residue at $z=0$) occurs at $n=1$:
$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}e^{i/z}=\frac{i}{1!}=i.$$

**Step 4: Apply the residue theorem.**
$$\oint_{|z|=1}e^{i/z}\,dz=2\pi i\cdot\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}e^{i/z}=2\pi i\cdot i=-2\pi.$$
Therefore
$$J=\frac{1}{i}(-2\pi)=2\pi i,$$
which is purely imaginary, so
$$I=\Re(J)=\Re(2\pi i)=0.$$

**Sanity check (symmetry).** Observe $f(\theta)=e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)$ satisfies
$$f(\pi-\theta)=e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\pi-\theta-\cos\theta)=-e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)=-f(\theta),$$
so $\int_{0}^{\pi}f=0$; likewise $f(-\pi-\theta)=-f(\theta)$ gives $\int_{-\pi}^{0}f=0$. Hence $I=0$, confirming the residue-theorem computation.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}e^{\sin\theta}\cos(\theta+\cos\theta)\,d\theta=0.}$$

